=== HINT 1: Install SA_SIGINFO handler ===
struct sigaction sa;
memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
sa.sa_sigaction = fpe_handler;
sa.sa_flags     = SA_SIGINFO;
sigaction(SIGFPE, &sa, NULL);

=== HINT 2: Trigger divide-by-zero ===
volatile int divisor = 0;
volatile int result  = 1 / divisor;

=== HINT 3: Inside handler - advance RIP ===
ucontext_t *uc = (ucontext_t *)uc_void;
// Skip the 2-byte idiv instruction (opcode F7 /7 or similar)
uc->uc_mcontext.gregs[REG_RIP] += 2;

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
SA_SIGINFO gives full context: siginfo_t tells you the faulting address (si_addr),
the signal code (FPE_INTDIV, SEGV_MAPERR, SEGV_ACCERR, etc.), and the sender PID.
ucontext_t lets you read and modify all CPU registers from the signal handler.
Advancing RIP is fragile - the instruction length varies.
libsigsegv uses a safer approach (longjmp or alternate stack recovery).
Sanitizers (ASan, UBSan) use SIGSEGV handlers to intercept memory violations
and print detailed reports before terminating.
